[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memory Management & Garbage Collection Optimization in Unity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding how Unity manages memory is critical for developing high-performance games. Unity utilizes both unmanaged memory (C++ core, textures, meshes) and managed memory (C# scripts, objects allocated on the heap). Garbage Collection (GC) operates exclusively on the managed heap, cleaning up unreferenced objects. Frequent GC allocations cause stuttering and frame drops."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Core Problem: Allocations on the Heap"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every time you use the 'new' keyword with reference types (like classes, strings, and arrays), memory is allocated on the managed heap. When this memory fills up, Unity triggers the Garbage Collector to scan the entire heap for unreferenced objects. This process pauses the main thread, leading to noticeable performance hiccups."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Reducing GC Allocations"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cache component references in Awake() or Start() instead of calling GetComponent inside Update()."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid string concatenations inside frequent loops. Use StringBuilder for heavy string manipulations."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be cautious with LINQ and anonymous methods, as they frequently cause hidden boxing allocations and closures."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use structs instead of classes for lightweight data containers to ensure they are allocated on the stack rather than the heap."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Object Pooling"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Object pooling is the most effective pattern to eliminate runtime allocations for frequently spawned items like projectiles, particles, and enemies. Instead of instantiating and destroying objects constantly, you preload a set number of objects and reuse them."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;

public class SimpleObjectPool : MonoBehaviour
{
    public GameObject prefab;
    private Queue<GameObject> pool = new Queue<GameObject>();

    public GameObject GetObject(Vector3 position, Quaternion rotation)
    {
        GameObject obj;
        if (pool.Count > 0)
        {
            obj = pool.Dequeue();
            obj.transform.position = position;
            obj.transform.rotation = rotation;
            obj.SetActive(true);
        }
        else
        {
            obj = Instantiate(prefab, position, rotation);
        }
        return obj;
    }

    public void ReturnObject(GameObject obj)
    {
        obj.SetActive(false);
        pool.Enqueue(obj);
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Incremental Garbage Collection"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unity provides an Incremental Garbage Collection mode, which spreads the GC workload across multiple frames rather than executing it all at once. You can enable this feature in Project Settings > Player > Other Settings by checking 'Use Incremental GC'. This drastically reduces maximum frame time spikes."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Profiling Your Memory"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Always verify your optimizations using the Unity Profiler. Open the Profiler window (Window > Analysis > Profiler), enable the CPU Usage and Memory modules, and look at the GC Alloc column. Track down any recurring spikes and refactor your C# scripts to achieve a smooth, allocation-free gameplay loop."}]
